            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for unworthy, then the Latin for to be indignant."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ōdī, ōdisse",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ōdī, ōdisse — \"to hate\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Fēlēs aquam ōdit. The cat hates water."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Perfect in form, present in meaning: ōdī — I hate, ōderam — I hated, ōderō — I shall hate. Future participle ōsūrus."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ōdī is inherited, through Proto-Italic ōdai, from a Proto-Indo-European root meaning \"to hate\" (EtymDB). The noun odium \"hatred\", source of English odium and odious, is built on it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
