        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: taedet, taedēre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "taedet, taedēre — \"it wearies, it disgusts\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Mē taedet longārum ōrātiōnum. I am weary of long speeches."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Impersonal: taedet, taedēre, taeduit or taesum est. The person in the accusative, the thing in the genitive: mē taedet labōris — I am sick of the work. Taedium, already known, is built on it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "taedet is a native Latin impersonal verb, and the noun taedium \"weariness\" is built on it (Lewis & Short); its deeper origin is not settled. English tedium and tedious come from taedium."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
